    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d663">

                <pb facs="0980" n="860" sameAs="frus:frus1922v01/pg860"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">493.11 Shelton, Dr. A. L.: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Schurman</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1922-03-04T19:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">March 4, 1922—7
                                p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received March 6—10 a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>28. Following telegram has been received from consul [at] Chungking: <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“Following telegram has been received from American missionary at Batang:
                            ‘Robbers killed Doctor A. L. Shelton<anchor n="25" xml:id="d663-f1"/>
                            near Batang. I have asked local government to investigate at once.
                            Department’s registration number 16539.’”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <p>Telegram dated 2d, signed Hardy<anchor n="26" xml:id="d663-f2"/> [apparent
                    omission] left Yunnan Province states Doctor Shelton killed February 17th at
                    Sasweisan. Have informed Foreign Office today requesting it to take immediately
                    all possible measures to ascertain circumstances of this reported crime and to
                    apprehend and bring to trial guilty parties. Shall reserve representations
                    particularly on the basis of Department [<hi rendition="#italic">diplomatic
                        corps’?</hi>] note to Foreign Office of August 30th, 1921,<anchor n="27"
                        xml:id="d663-f3"/> regarding possible failure of provincial authorities to
                        <pb facs="0981" n="861" xml:id="pg861"/> afford proper protection to foreign
                    residents until facts and delinquencies of local officials are more clearly
                    ascertained. British consul, Yunnan; representative, Andersen, Meyer, Yunnan;
                    and American consulate, Chungking, have been requested to furnish all possible
                    assistance. Will keep Department advised.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Schurman</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d663-f1" n="25" xml:id="d663-f1-content">
                <p>American missionary of the Tibetan Christian Mission, Batang, Szechuan
                    Province.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d663-f2" n="26" xml:id="d663-f2-content">
                <p>Dr. William M. Hardy, colleague of Dr. Shelton.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d663-f3" n="27" xml:id="d663-f3-content">
                <p>See despatch no. 45, Sept. 29, 1921, from the Minister in China, <ref
                        target="frus1921v01#pg_516"><hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>,
                        1921, vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps">i</hi>, p. 516</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
